{#
Generated by: Claude Code CLI
Date time: 2026/09/07 15:00 SAST
Version: 0.6
Description:
  The one page header on the platform.

  Every screen answers the same four questions in the same place: where am I in the platform, what is
  this page called, what is it for, and how do I get back. Before this each screen answered them in
  its own markup, so the answers moved from page to page and three of them were simply missing on
  seventeen screens.

  The parts, and why each is optional rather than required:

  The back link is present wherever there is somewhere specific to go back to. A page reached from
  one place has an obvious parent; a section reached from a menu does not, and a back link pointing
  at a guess is worse than none.

  The kicker names the part of the platform the reader is in. It is what makes a page title like
  "Courses" unambiguous, because there is more than one Courses screen and they belong to different
  workspaces.

  The lead says what the screen is for in one sentence. It is the only part a reader can skip
  entirely once they know the screen, which is why it is last in the block and never carries anything
  they need.

  The action is the one thing a reader most often wants next from here. One, not a row: a header full
  of buttons is a toolbar, and this is a heading.

  Compact mode exists for the course player and nothing else. A course carries its own title, its own
  progress and its own navigation, and a full platform header above that competes with the thing the
  reader came for. Compact keeps the back link and the title on one line and drops the rest.

  Core owns this markup. Themes style it through the page-head classes.

  WARNING: a template comment is not a safe place to document syntax. A comment delimiter
  written inside a comment body ends the comment early and leaks the remainder onto the page.
  Keep every comment plain prose: no comment delimiters and no code, not even to document one.

Changelog:
  2026/09/07 15:00 SAST
  - Created so every screen carries the same header in the same place.
#}
{% if ph_compact is defined and ph_compact %}
	<section class="cl-page-head cl-page-head-compact">
		<div class="container-fluid cl-container-wide py-3">
			<div class="cl-page-head-row">
				{% if ph_back_href is defined and ph_back_href %}
					<a class="cl-back-link" href="{{ ph_back_href }}">← {{ ph_back_label is defined and ph_back_label ? ph_back_label : 'Back' }}</a>
				{% endif %}
				<h1 class="h5 mb-0">
					{{ ph_title }}
				</h1>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
{% else %}
	<section class="cl-page-head">
		<div class="container-fluid cl-container-wide py-5">
			<div class="d-flex flex-column flex-lg-row justify-content-between align-items-lg-end gap-3">
				<div>
					{% if ph_back_href is defined and ph_back_href %}
						<a class="cl-back-link" href="{{ ph_back_href }}">← {{ ph_back_label is defined and ph_back_label ? ph_back_label : 'Back' }}</a>
					{% endif %}
					{% if ph_kicker is defined and ph_kicker %}
						<span class="cl-kicker d-block {{ ph_back_href is defined and ph_back_href ? 'mt-3' : '' }}">{{ ph_kicker }}</span>
					{% endif %}
					<h1 class="display-5 fw-bold">
						{{ ph_title }}
					</h1>
					{% if ph_lead is defined and ph_lead %}
						<p class="lead mb-0">
							{{ ph_lead }}
						</p>
					{% endif %}
				</div>
				{% if ph_action_href is defined and ph_action_href %}
					<a class="btn btn-light" href="{{ ph_action_href }}">{{ ph_action_label is defined and ph_action_label ? ph_action_label : 'Back' }}</a>
				{% endif %}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
{% endif %}
{#
	The identity band belongs to the head rather than to the page, so it is included here instead of
	by each page. That is also what keeps it directly beneath the head: a page that included it
	itself could put it anywhere, and most pages never included it at all.
#}
